#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses.

** TODO The inserts of =deque= against 23.2.1.3, p1 and p2

23.2.1.3, p2: if an exception is thrown other than by the copy
constructor or assignment operator of =T=, =insert= has no effects.
The input iterator overload of =__rw_insert_range= in
=include/deque.cc= meets it since the whole-copy branch came back:
a copy of the container with the range inserted, swapped in on
success. The bidirectional overload, =_C_insert_n= and the
single-element insert into the middle do not: they push the new
elements onto the container with =push_front= and =push_back= before
they shuffle them into place, and a node allocation that fails
mid-way leaves the container grown. The deque allocates its buffers
one at a time between element constructions, which is where the
=bad_alloc= lands.

23.2.1.3, p1: an insert at either end leaves references to the
elements valid. The whole-copy branch moves every element, so it
meets p2 at the price of p1 at the ends; the one-at-a-time insert it
replaced met p1 and not p2. No shape meets both without allocating
ahead.

The way that meets both, decided 2026-10-16: every slot of the
node vector owns a buffer, so the vector's extent is the storage
and =_C_beg= and =_C_end= mark the live part of it; what lies
between the vector's ends and the live range is reserved, and the
destructor frees every slot. An insert of n at an end then grows the
node vector once if the reserve on that side is short, allocates the
buffers for the new slots before any element is touched, freeing
them if one allocation fails, and constructs into the reserve. After
the growth nothing allocates, so the only exceptions left are =T='s,
which the clause exempts, and a failed growth leaves nothing
observable; a successful one leaves the reserve. Buffers are
allocated by need: an insert of n takes the buffers the spare on its
side is short, rotating spare buffers from the far end first, since
a buffer is a buffer; the ratio stays on the node vector, which
keeps =__rw_new_capacity='s golden ratio and minimum when it
regrows. A ratio on the buffers would buy nothing: the deque's unit
of growth is already a buffer, so each allocation serves
=_C_bufsize ()= pushes, where =vector='s unit is one element.
libstdc++ and libc++ both double the map and allocate blocks by
need; libc++ also keeps every block it allocates, as decided here,
and releases spare blocks on pops only beyond one per end. One
mechanism for the three overloads; the input iterator overload then
becomes a temporary handed to the bidirectional one and the whole
copy goes. The spare goes to the end nearest the insertion point.
=clear= keeps the map, as =vector='s keeps its capacity; only the
destructor releases it, and the no-map state remains the
default-constructed one.

What the shape changes: the null slot on each side of the live
range, written by =_C_push= and the frees and asserted by
=_C_is_valid=, goes, the frees no longer release a buffer when the
live range leaves it, and the growth test in =_C_push= (end's node
is the last slot) becomes the reserve trigger. The iterator
arithmetic does not read what the neighbouring slot holds:
equality is positional, =0 == x - y=, and the difference reads
=x._C_cur - *x._C_node=, zero for an iterator stepped onto any
slot. The map's growth today is in slots, minimum 32, placed at a
quarter or half of the new vector, and stays; in buffers that policy
would hold 4 KiB for one =int=, which is why buffers go by need.

=tests/containers/23.deque.modifiers.cpp= runs its exception loop for
the input iterator range insert at every position and length; for
the other inserts it still turns exceptions off into the middle and
for more than one element, which is where they fail.

Fix: in the tree, in three commits, each gated on the suite. First
the storage model with behaviour unchanged: every slot owns a
buffer, the sentinel nulls go, the frees stop releasing, =clear=
keeps the map, the destructor walks the whole vector, =_C_is_valid=
asserts the new invariant; every row of the suite equal to the pin,
counts included. Second the reserve and the three inserts, gated on
the exception loop with its exemptions removed. Third the missing
assertion below. Nothing outside =include/deque= and
=include/deque.cc= reads the internals; =queue= and =stack= only
name the type. A driver against the tree's headers gives the fast
loop, the three =23.deque= tests the verdict.

A pop never frees the buffer it empties (decided 2026-10-16). Today
it frees it, which thrashes across a block boundary; libc++ frees
beyond one spare block per end. Here the map holds the peak until
the deque is destroyed or swapped: storage is released by the
=deque<T> ().swap (d)= idiom, which already works, since =swap=
exchanges the map. =shrink_to_fit= is C++11 and not in the
interface.

Check: =23.deque.modifiers= with the two exemptions removed, and an
assertion that a reference into the deque survives an insert at
either end, which the suite does not have.

** TODO The constructors of =vector= when =assign= throws

The three constructing constructors of =vector= in =include/vector=
are an =assign= call with no handler, the shape the =deque= ones had
until their fix: an object whose constructor throws is never
destroyed, so whatever =assign= had built is lost. For =deque= the
loss was measured. For =vector= the hypothesis is that nothing is
lost: =assign= on an empty vector is an insert, and the vector insert
builds into a temporary and swaps, so a throw leaves the object
empty and owning nothing.

Fix: none if the hypothesis holds; the =deque= handler otherwise.

Check: =23.vector.modifiers='s leak counter around the three
constructors under injected =bad_alloc= and a throwing copy
constructor, the way the =deque= test's loop now runs.

** TODO Leftovers beside the removed function probes

The removal of the C library function probes (f43be017..a44256a6)
kept to the code that read the probe macros. Its census found dead
or stale code beside them. No tracked configuration compiles any of
it differently.

- =_RWSTD_NO_USING_LIBC_IN_STD= is defined nowhere. Fifteen headers
  test it around their =namespace std= blocks.
- =src/file.cpp= declares =fileno= under
  =#ifndef _RWSTD_NO_PURE_C_HEADERS=, which the file defines above;
  the block cannot be compiled.
- =_cstdio.h= declares =gets= in =std= and =stdio.h= exports it, both
  in pure-headers mode only. The default mode leaves it out, as
  C++14 does.
- =_RWSTD_FMT_TYPE=, =_RWSTD_FMT_ASSGN=, =_STRTOLL= and =_STRTOULL=
  in =_defs.h= have had no user since the 2005 import.
  =_RWSTD_WCSFTIME_ARG3_T= has no reader outside its
  characterization. =config.h= carries =_RWSTD_NO_ABS_INT=; nothing
  reads it.
- =<cmath>='s =_FABSF= calls =fabsf= without the =::= of its 43
  siblings. The 44 =_<F>F= and =_<F>L= macros could be inlined into
  the overloads that call them.
- =21.cwchar= exercises none of =wcscoll=, =wcsncat=, =vswprintf=,
  =vwprintf=, =getwchar=, =putwchar=.
- =libc_decl.sh= only looks for headers now; its name says more.
  =headers.inc= holds one list, for one script.
- Hypothesis: =__rw_get_timepunct= calls =wcsftime= with wide literal
  formats whatever =_RWSTD_NO_WCSFTIME_WCHAR_T_FMAT= says; on a C
  library whose third parameter is =const char*= it would not
  compile. Check: =git log -L= on those calls for a platform note.

Fix: one commit per item, each inert on the six configurations
except the =21.cwchar= coverage, which adds assertions.

Check: the per-commit check of the removal (=config.h=, object code,
the non-debug recompile, the suite table) in =11S=.

** TODO The =_RWSTD_REBIND= macro and the allocator shims beside it

=_RWSTD_REBIND (from, to)= in =include/rw/_defs.h= spells
=typename from::template rebind<to>::other= at eleven sites in
=deque=, =list=, =string=, =vector= and =_tree.h=. It exists for two
compilers the matrix no longer has: one without the =template=
keyword in a dependent name, =_RWSTD_NO_DEPENDENT_TEMPLATE=, which
Sun's did not accept, and one without member templates at all,
where the macro names =allocator_interface= instead. The second gate
is =_RWSTD_ALLOCATOR=, defined unless
=_RWSTD_NO_SIMPLE_DEFAULT_TEMPLATES= is, and nothing under
=etc/config/src= characterizes that macro, so the
=allocator_interface= side has been dead since the import.
=_RWSTD_ALLOC_TYPE=, =_RWSTD_VALUE_ALLOC= and =_RWSTD_VALUE_ALLOC_CAST=
are the same workaround, 25 sites and none respectively, and
=allocator_interface= itself, in =_allocator.h= and
=_specialized.h=, is its target. The rebind macro was a workaround
for an ancient Sun compiler; remove it (2026-10-16).

Fix: spell the rebind at the eleven sites, delete the macro and its
=_RWSTD_NO_DEPENDENT_TEMPLATE= branch; then the three sibling macros
and the =_RWSTD_ALLOCATOR= gate with the =allocator_interface= side,
and =DEPENDENT_TEMPLATE.cpp= once nothing reads its macro.

Check: the per-commit check of the removal (=config.h=, object code,
the non-debug recompile, the suite table) in =11S=.

** TODO Dead members and sources of the 4.2.x layout

The revival keeps neither source nor binary compatibility with
4.2.x; the next version is 5.0, named from the state of the library
when it is cut.
Two things stayed only for that compatibility:
- The =numpunct= cache members, unused since 2e196ebb
  (=include/loc/_numpunct.h:117=).
- =src/x86/atomic.s=, =src/x86_64/atomic.s= and the symbols they
  export; no header has referred to them since the backend moved to
  the =__atomic= built-ins.

Fix: remove the members, remove the sources and their symbols.

Check: the exported symbols lose exactly the assembly routines. The
suites are otherwise unchanged.

** TODO =time_get::do_date_order= returns =no_order= in every locale

The base facet returns =dateorder ()= and no byname facet overrides
it, since the 2005 import. 22.2.5.1.2, p1 asks for the order of the
components of the =x= format when it is composed of day, month and
year, and footnote 233 allows =no_order= "in valid locales", so the
library conforms. The =time_get= example prints =date_order ()= in
the =C= locale, whose =x= is =%m/%d/%y=, and its expected output has
said =mdy= since the import, so the example has shown =DIFF= on
every platform the tree ever ran on. The suite does not assert
=date_order= anywhere.

Fix: decide. Either =do_date_order= reads the facet's =x= format and
answers =dmy=, =mdy=, =ymd= or =ydm= when the format is one of the
four, the convenience the footnote describes, or the expected output
takes =0= and the example documents what the library does.

Check: the =time_get= example row is clean, and =22.locale.time.get=
asserts =date_order= for the =C= locale and one named locale.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** TODO The wide half of UserCtype

=tests/src/ctype.cpp= implements =UserCtype<wchar_t>= and
=UserCtype<UserChar>= in part: ten members are "FIXME: implement
this" stubs. =make_char= (=tests/include/rw_char.h=) takes only a
=char=. =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp= stops its table at =UCHAR_MAX=, so
the wide entries have never run.

Fix: finish the helper, give =make_char= an =int= overload, remove the
break.

Check: =0.ctype= exercises the whole table for all three character
types.

** TODO MT tests that provoke contention instead of waiting for it

The older =22.locale.*.mt= tests run one thread per processor, up to
32, for 10,000 to 200,000 iterations each. Each threaded section runs
to its own 60-second soft timeout. After the first iteration
everything they touch is cached, and the rest measures the locks at
rest. On 32 processors seven of them outlast the harness's 60-second
timeout. The address-space limit grows with the processor count in
thread-safe builds and no longer stops them. Each test covers several
facets, so a failure names a test, not a defect.

The four gated tests, =22.locale.use_facet.mt=,
=22.locale.time.put.libc.mt=, =22.locale.id.mt= and
=22.locale.facet.mt=, find their races in seconds. A few threads wait
on a barrier and start together at the first use of a fresh object,
round after round. They overlap the older tests.

A plain run catches a race only when the kernel's scheduling puts two
threads in its window, and that is the same for every run on one
kernel and changes with the next. ThreadSanitizer reports a race from
what ran, not from its timing, and is the judge from here.

Fix: first, with no change to the sources, run each program under the
sanitizer at decreasing sizes through its own options, against the
fixed library and with its fault put back. The smallest size that
still reports the fault, and nothing on the fixed library, becomes
the program's default. =doc/notes/analysis-locale-mt-sizing.md=
records the method and each program's runs and times. Then a census
of what each older test covers against the gated ones: each kept,
split or retired with the reason, one race or one facet operation per
program. Plain runs are sized for speed: a few threads whatever the
processor count, short rounds on fresh state, the timeout a hang
detector.

State: all 18 programs are sized in 15D and pass in 12D (the note's
chapter 5); the sizes are the programs' defaults, overridable through
their options. The note's chapter 4 holds what the census needs:
=22.locale.cons.mt= reaches no fixed fault (it never fills a facet
slot); the classic section of =22.locale.statics.mt= races a classic
locale its static array already built; the leak checks of
=22.locale.time.put.libc.mt= and =22.locale.use_facet.mt= are the only
detectors of their faults; =22.locale.money.put.mt= drops =C.utf8=
without a word; six fixed commits were not put back; in 12D
=22.locale.messages.mt= reports the slot fault in 86 runs of 100. In
the full GCC run at bf5fbd46 every locale MT test passes under the
harness in 15D in about a second on 32 processors, and the GCC tables
pin the rows (=ref-test-baseline.md=, chapter 3.2); the Clang tables
are not yet measured at the new sizes.

Check: every program in the note's table has a chosen size, a time,
and runs under the sanitizer that report its fault and stay clean on
the fixed library, in 15D and in 12D. Every locale MT test passes in
seconds under the harness in 15D on any processor count, and the 15D
rows become a reference.

** TODO All the C library's locales on the test hosts

The test hosts generate one glibc locale besides =C= and =POSIX=:
=en_US.UTF-8=. Tests that loop over the installed locales see four.
Three examples fail on it outright: =numpunct= finds none of its
German names and ends =DIFF=, =codecvt1= wants =ja_JP.UTF-8=,
=ja_JP.EUC-JP= and =ja_JP.Shift_JIS= and exits 1, =money_get= wants
=en_US= and aborts on the uncaught =runtime_error=. =SUPPORTED= has
no Shift_JIS locale, the charmap not being ASCII-compatible; glibc
builds one by hand all the same, ~localedef -c -f SHIFT_JIS -i ja_JP
ja_JP.SHIFT_JIS~, with a warning and exit status 1. The examples run against the C
library's locales alone; the build's own databases are not built
for them and =RWSTD_LOCALE_ROOT= is not set. Installing every locale
in =/usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED= widens them, but it also teaches the
C library =de_DE.ISO-8859-1=, the name =rw_localedef= gives the
database locale that the first-use section of =22.locale.codecvt.mt=
races. The section then skips with a note and passes without testing
anything. The =numpunct= example's list of German names still
carries the spellings of the retired platforms, AIX to Windows, and
the error it reports names the last of them. One host has every
locale since 2026-10-14, =SUPPORTED= through =locale-gen= and the
Shift_JIS one by hand: the three examples run clean there, and
=22.locale.codecvt.mt= skips its first-use section there with the
note. The other test hosts have =en_US.UTF-8= alone. On that host
=22.locale.codecvt= reaches 53 assertions the pin never saw, the
sections gated on the C library knowing a named locale, and fails
35 of them: =do_length= against the C library's count for
=ja_JP.UTF-8=, =ja_JP.EUC-JP= and =ja_JP.Shift_JIS= (line 611), and
the wide =in= and =out= checks at lines 1520 to 1596. A binary
built before the locales were installed fails the same 35, so the
host, not the library's change of the day, is the variable; the
cause is not yet read.

Fix: the tests that race a database locale build it under a name the
C library cannot know (=rw_localedef='s fourth argument), then every
locale is installed on all test hosts at once, and the pins are
measured again.

Check: with every locale installed, no MT program notes that the C
library knows its database locale, and the =numpunct=, =codecvt1=
and =money_get= examples run clean.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

* Certification

The scripts under =bin= and the nightly run: building the
configurations, running their suites and comparing the tables with
their pins.

** TODO The scripts under =bin= drive the certification

=bin/buildntest= builds and runs one configuration and writes a log
that describes itself. =bin/xbuildgen= hands a night's logs and
=etc/config/xfail.txt= to =bin/xcomp.awk=, which writes one HTML page
with a column per build. A run of 15D at 3d90080b went through all
three unmodified. The certification has run on a private script with
one-line summaries since September.

Fix:
- The repair pass, one defect per commit. Among the defects: =-v=
  takes an argument and =-u= is rejected; =-o= is ignored; the run
  stage reports the tests stage's counts; =date= runs in the caller's
  locale and breaks =bin/duration=; =free -o=, =hostname= and =gmake=
  are gone from Linux; the svn keywords and links are dead;
  =xcomp.awk= knows no aarch64.
- =bin/driver= runs one =buildntest= per configuration, each in its
  own build directory, with logs named as =xcomp.awk= expects, and
  writes one page per build into its build directory. Left: each
  tests table compared with its pin, one verdict and an exit status.
  =norm.sh= and =derive-pin.sh= come into the tree beside it. The
  driver takes GCC build types only; the three Clang configurations
  run by hand through the same =buildntest= call with =CXX=clang= on
  the make line, and belong in =-c=.
- The certification runs the tests only; the locale and example
  phases are not pinned. =buildntest= takes empty lists for them
  (=-e ''= and =-l ''=), which saves the locale phase's 22 minutes per
  configuration, and =xcomp.awk= must file the tests table of such a
  log as tests: a bare =make run= log comes out under locales.
- The page stays local. The logs carry the environment, the host
  name and =/proc/cpuinfo=, and nothing scrubs them.

Check: one night of the six configurations through the driver gives
a page per build, the six pins identical, and a verdict that fails
when a pin is perturbed.

** TODO Run the suites in parallel

The six suites run one after another, about 26 minutes on 32
processors. 14 of them are timeouts: seven older locale MT tests run
into the harness's 60 seconds in each thread-safe suite. The rest is a
minute or two per suite, most of it in a few long tests. The suites
are already isolated from each other: separate build directories,
each program's output beside it, and a =TMPDIR= of its own for every
=make run= (=etc/config/makefile.rules=). They share only the
processors, and only the 15D MT rows depend on the load.

Fix, in order:
- The MT entry under Tests. It removes the timeouts and the load
  sensitivity.
- The configurations' suites at once: the four single-threaded ones
  now, the thread-safe ones with them once the MT entry lands. A
  command for =doc/notes/ref-test-baseline.md=, not a tool to
  maintain.
- One configuration's list split across several harness runs, only if
  a single-threaded suite grows past a few minutes. Within one run the
  tests share =TMPDIR= and the locales =localedef= builds; each shard
  needs its own, and the census of what else they share comes first.

Check: the six tables of a parallel run identical to those of a
serial one, three runs over; the wall time recorded beside the tables.

* Locales

The locale sources and charmaps under =etc/nls=, the =localedef= that
compiles them and the databases the facets read.

** TODO The locale sources under =etc/nls= are glibc 2.2's

The definitions and charmaps under =etc/nls= are copies from GNU libc
2.2, as the original =README= says, last changed in 312ef830 (2007).
A survey on 2026-10-13 compared them with glibc's =localedata= at
60a2ae4098 (2.44.9000, 2026-09-29). Coverage: 179 files against 372,
163 language and territory definitions against 351. 139 names match,
20 more once the =.euro= suffix maps to =@euro=; four names are
legacy and ours only (=iw_IL=, =no_NO=, =sr_YU=, =sr_YU.cyrillic=);
192 are glibc's only. Among the 139 shared definitions, after
resolving =copy= directives: 46 currencies differ (=ro_RO= ROL to
RON, =fr_FR= FRF to EUR, =tr_TR= TRL to TRY), 75 currency symbols,
54 thousands separators, 8 decimal points, 45 groupings; =d_fmt=
differs in 36, the month names in 45, the weekday names in 24;
=ja_JP= has no Reiwa era; =ro_RO= collates circumflex before breve
where glibc now does the opposite. The shared tables are older still:
=i18n= says Unicode 3.0, the UTF-8 charmap's widths Unicode 3.2,
against Unicode 14.0.0 in glibc's =i18n_ctype=; =U+1C90=, =U+1E900=
and =U+1F600= are absent from the charmap. =localedef= cannot read
the current sources: glibc's =en_US= with its UTF-8 charmap exits 4,
Error 312, symbolic name =<S02D0>= not found at =iso14651_t1_common=
line 63100, and the keyword table in =util/scanner.cpp= lacks
=alt_mon=, =ab_alt_mon=, =class=, =map= and the conditionals. The
survey compared source values and ran no collation or formatting
oracle, so the counts are differences, not proven defects. The GNU
categories outside the six of C++ (paper, address, measurement) are
not a gap. =doc/notes/analysis-locale-sources.md= holds the survey. The facets can also read the C library's own locales
(=doc/notes/ref-locale.md=), so stale sources need not reach every
=std::locale= a program constructs.

Fix: in order. First the compiler: find what in the current sources
it rejects, the Error 312 first, and extend the scanner and the =Def=
writers until glibc's =en_US= builds. Then the shared tables together
with their charmaps, =i18n=, the transliteration and collation
templates: refreshed leaf definitions alone leave the shared tables
stale. Then the definitions, the legacy names kept through an
explicit mapping. Then the databases regenerated and the decoded
facet values compared with the glibc source baseline, the currency,
numeric and date repairs staged separately.

Check: =localedef= builds every definition in the refreshed
=etc/nls/src= with its charmap. The locale tests pass against their
pins, re-pinned where the data moved. The decoded currency, numeric
and date values of the 139 shared definitions match the glibc
sources they come from.

* EDG porting

The tree builds with EDG's =eccp= in strict mode (=-A=) as a
conformance check: a question is settled there before a bug goes to
another compiler. The front end is open source as of 2026-09:
github.com/edgcpp/compiler. The first entry is the build; the rest
are what it found. Each error is read against the standard: a library
defect is fixed here, a compiler defect goes to EDG, and the leniency
of GCC or Clang it exposes is recorded. The =WAITING= entries at the
end are defects reported to EDG or glibc.

A first build on 2026-10-06, at 6bb238b6 with EDG 7.0 over gcc 16 and
glibc, took hand changes to the tree. It configured, built the
library, the test driver and the utilities, and built 257 of 272 test
programs. The suite ran without signals or non-zero exits: 10,049,711
assertions, 16 failed. A second build on 2026-10-08, at 48d6dac1 after
the retirement of =export=, with the same hand changes and the same
front end, gave the same table.

** TODO The =eccp= configuration

The platform retirement removed =etc/config/eccp.config= (dd1128a5),
=include/rw/_config-eccp.h= (6513a431) and the prelink step before
=ar= (67c1f7b6; its message says only the retired compilers used it,
but =eccp.config= did). The =__EDG__= guards went with the platform
retirement (d743fa8d, 12475dfe); comments that name =eccp= survive in
=_iosbase.h=, =_traits.h= and five characterizations, and so do the
=eccp= rows of =etc/config/xfail.txt=.

The default dialect of =eccp= is C++03 (=__cplusplus= is =199711L=).
No dialect option is needed. The C headers are a property of the
=eccp= installation, not of the tree: it searches the resident gcc's
freestanding headers and glibc's, and its macro table carries the
target facts gcc's =float.h= and =limits.h= read.

gcc's =stddef.h= spells =offsetof= as =__builtin_offsetof=, a keyword
EDG enters only when it emulates GNU, Microsoft or Clang. The name is
reserved, so strict mode may provide it; reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/185. A =-D= cannot
carry the stopgap: =libc_decl.sh= expands =$CPPFLAGS= unquoted, the
shell splits the definition at its spaces, and every header probe
fails. A file passed with =--preinclude_macros= works:
~#define __builtin_offsetof(T, M) ((__SIZE_TYPE__) __INTADDR__ (&((T *) 0)->M))~.
=__INTADDR__= is EDG's own =offsetof= primitive. The file leaves when
EDG enters the keyword.

Fix: restore =eccp.config= from =dd1128a5^= and bring it current:
=-A -x=, =--no_export= while the =WAITING= =src/punct.cpp= entry stands,
the prelinker (~PRELINKFLAGS = --prelink_objects~, the step in
=GNUmakefile.lib= and =GNUmakefile.rwt=, =*.ti= in the clean rules),
=-lpthread=, the stopgap file. A macro of the override header
comes back only where no characterization can express the gap, with
its reason.

Check: an =eccp= build directory configures, builds the library, the
utilities and the tests, and runs the suite. Its table is pinned
beside the six.

** TODO Run-time differences from GCC

Under =eccp -A=:
- =26.valarray.transcend=: nine failed assertions on the =float=
  functions (=std::acos (valarray<float>)= and kin); =config.h= has
  no =float= or =long double= overloads of the =<cmath>= functions.
- =18.limits.traps=: =numeric_limits<bool>::traps= is true, as for
  every integer type, but the test's =bool= division by zero did not
  trap. Outside the test, =bool= division by zero traps under =eccp=
  too. The same row fails in an optimized GCC build (12D, 13
  assertions) and once in 12D with Clang: the test counts on undefined
  behavior surviving the optimizer, which is the first suspect.
- =18.support.dynamic=: =bad_alloc::what ()= returns a bad string.
  The first question is whose =bad_alloc= runs, the library's or that
  of EDG's runtime.
- =22.locale.num.put= runs 1,399 assertions against 1,485.

Fix: read each against the standard and the characterization that
decides it.

Check: the rows pass, or the reason is recorded.

** TODO The configuration under =eccp= against GCC's

=config.h= under =eccp -A= differs from GCC's 11S beyond the other
entries of this chapter: no atomic built-ins and no =_RWSTD_THREAD=; =STD_TYPE_INFO=
fails (the characterization declares its own =type_info=; 12475dfe
removed the =#pragma define_type_info= that served =eccp=); the
special-member characterizations of the exception classes differ.
=long long= and extern templates are absent, which is correct for
C++03.

Fix: read each difference against the standard and the
characterization.

Check: every difference is correct for C++03 or has an entry.

** TODO The C99 functions the library calls

The library calls =vsnprintf= (=src/exception.cpp=), =strtof= and
=strtold= (=src/num_get.cpp=) and =snprintf= (=src/num_put.cpp=).
C++03's C library is C90 with Amendment 1; none of the four is in it.
Under =eccp -A= glibc does not declare them. Each had a fallback
until 8760bf55: =vsprintf=, =strtod= clamped to =float=, =sscanf=.
Its premise, that every tracked configuration takes the C library
function, held for GCC and Clang only.

The first build exposed them with
~-D_XOPEN_SOURCE=500 -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE~ in =eccp.config=. That is a pin and cannot stay.

Fix: undecided. The candidates: declare the functions where they are
used, as =FLOAT.cpp= did; restore the fallbacks behind a
characterization; or let the sources that need them state their
feature level, as =tests/src/file.cpp= does.

Check: the library builds under =eccp -A= with no feature macro on
the command line, and the GCC and Clang tables do not move.

** TODO =FLOAT.cpp= under a strict compiler

The =FLOAT= characterization calls =strtof= and =strtold=, which
strict C++03 does not declare. It fails, =config.h= has no
=_RWSTD_FLT_*= macros, and =<limits>= does not compile. a44256a6
removed the characterization's own declarations.

Fix: declare the two functions in the characterization again, or
probe underflow with =strtod= alone.

Check: =FLOAT= passes under =eccp -A= and =config.h= carries the
=_RWSTD_FLT_*= macros; the GCC and Clang =config.h= do not change.

** TODO =__CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO= needs GNU =__asm= labels

=include/ansi/cwchar= and =include/ansi/wchar.h= define it
unconditionally. glibc declares the overloads behind it with =__asm=
labels, which strict mode rejects: 60 errors in six sources. Guarded
on =__GNUC__=, the sources compile and =21.cwchar= finds the C
prototypes only: five failed assertions on the const overloads.

Fix: define the macro for compilers that accept glibc's declarations,
and give the others the const-correct pair some other way.

Check: the library builds under =eccp -A=; =21.cwchar= passes there
and its GCC and Clang rows do not move.

** TODO Tests that GCC and Clang let through

Ten test programs do not compile under =eccp -A= for causes in the
test:
- =typeid= without =<typeinfo>=, ill-formed (5.2.8p6):
  =tests/support/18.exception.cpp:883=,
  =tests/support/18.support.rtti.cpp:99=.
- =std::size_t= without =<cstddef>=: =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp:51=,
  =tests/localization/22.locale.facet.mt.cpp:130=.
- No newline at the end of the file:
  =tests/numerics/26.gslice.array.cassign.cpp=.
- =long long= unguarded: =tests/iostream/27.istream.fmat.arith.cpp:1028=.
- POSIX names without a feature macro: =getcwd= in
  =tests/localization/22.locale.messages.cpp:616=, =BC_BASE_MAX= in
  =tests/regress/18.c.limits.stdcxx-988.cpp:53=.
- A UTF-8 =à= in a narrow string literal, which =eccp= rejects as not
  representable (#3439); the mapping is implementation-defined:
  =tests/regress/22.locale.collate.stdcxx-1010.cpp:71=.
- Template instantiation 435 deep:
  =tests/localization/22.locale.id.mt.cpp:98=. C++03 Annex B
  recommends at least 17.

Fix: repair each test; the instantiation depth is a decision of its
own.

Check: each compiles under =eccp -A=, and the GCC and Clang tables do
not move.

** TODO Calls EDG matches to no function

Four test programs fail with error #304, no instance of a function
template or overloaded function matches the argument list. Each call
is dependent and each callee it means has internal linkage.
- =tests/iostream/27.std.manip.cpp:518= (=test<charT, Traits>=),
  =tests/iostream/27.filebuf.cpp:2019= (=test_ctors<charT>=),
  =tests/localization/22.locale.codecvt.cpp:680=
  (=test_libstd_do_in<CodeCvtT>=): calls by template-id to =static=
  function templates. Six lines reproduce it: a dependent call to a
  =static= function template, by template-id or deduced, fails under
  =eccp -A= and compiles under =eccp --c++11 -A=, =-x= and GCC's
  =-std=c++98=. C++03 14.6.4.2 finds only functions with external
  linkage for a dependent call by unqualified-id; C++11 dropped the
  restriction. Whether the C++03 rule covers a template-id is to be
  read in the C++03 text; if it does not, the rejection is an EDG
  defect.
- =tests/localization/22.locale.collate.cpp:1159=: an unqualified call
  =check_NUL_collate (charTname, locname, charT ())=. EDG considers
  only the six-argument template; the =static= non-template overloads
  the call means (=:1072=, =:1108=) are not candidates. Small programs
  of the same shape compile under =eccp -A=, so the cause is not yet
  known; C-Vise is the next step.

Fix: read the C++03 text; then either give the callees external
linkage or report to EDG.

Check: each compiles under =eccp -A=, and the GCC and Clang tables do
not move.

** TODO The ISO-8859-1 sources

=eccp= reads source as UTF-8 only. Five sources carry ISO-8859-1
bytes: =etc/config/src/locale_names.h= (=LOCALE_NAME_FMAT= fails, so
no =_RWSTD_LC_*= constants), =tests/algorithms/25.search.cpp=,
=tests/iostream/27.istream.readsome.cpp=,
=tests/iostream/27.istream.sentry.cpp=,
=tests/localization/22.locale.money.get.cpp=. Only the first broke
the first build. An octal escape is the same byte to every
compiler.

Fix: octal escapes.

Check: the five are UTF-8 clean (=iconv -f utf-8=) and the tables do
not move.

** WAITING =src/punct.cpp= under the export model

The library uses neither =export= nor implicit inclusion since
11b144b9..48d6dac1. =eccp -A= still turns its export model on, and
under it EDG fails an internal assertion in =src/punct.cpp= at line
113 (#2656, in =find_local_static_variable_init=). The other 97
library sources compile. The 136 errors of the first build, 98 of them
"already been defined" in the =.cc= files, came from =_EXPORT= and are
gone. Reported as https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/233,
with a one-line candidate patch: a block scope that lowering adds for
a return-value temporary has no parent.

Fix: EDG's. Until then =eccp.config= passes =--no_export=.

Check: the =eccp= build needs no =--no_export= with a front end that
fixes it.

** WAITING =_setjmp= in the C that EDG generates for =25.libc=

=eccp= compiles C++ to C and gcc compiles the C. For
=tests/algorithms/25.libc.cpp= the generated C declares =_setjmp= as
=int (long *)=; glibc's =<setjmp.h>= declares it
=int (struct __jmp_buf_tag *)=. gcc rejects the conflict at
=25.libc.cpp:118=, and the test does not build under =eccp -A=.

A defect of the front end's C back end, in every mode: a translation
unit that calls =setjmp= and has a =try= block. The =try= lowering
declares its own =_setjmp= and does not look for the program's.
glibc spells =setjmp (env)= as =_setjmp (env)=. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/234.

Fix: EDG's. The test and the library need no change.

Check: =25.libc= builds under =eccp -A= with a front end that fixes
it.

** WAITING Names the prelinker cannot decode

The prelinker reports "an error occurred during name decoding" 22
times while it builds the library under =eccp -A=. c++filt decodes
the same names. All 22 have one shape, e.g.
=_ZN4__rw12__rw_extractIcSt11char_traitsIcEtEERSt13basic_istreamIT_T0_ES7_RT1_=,
=__rw::__rw_extract<char, std::char_traits<char>, unsigned short>=.
The test programs draw it for about 2,100 more.

A defect of EDG's tools, not of these names: on Linux =edg_decode=
decodes no IA-64 mangled name at all, and the prelinker warns for
every such name that contains =__=. A two-line program with a
function template in a namespace =__n= reproduces it. The prelinker
uses decoded names only in its messages; template instances are
assigned and the library builds. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/240.

Fix: EDG's. The library needs no change.

Check: a build with a front end that fixes it shows no decoding
warnings.

** WAITING =27.ostream=: string literals defined twice in the C that EDG generates

=tests/iostream/27.ostream.cpp= does not build under =eccp -A=. gcc
rejects the generated C: it defines the string literals of
=test_formatted<charT>= twice (=_ZZ14test_formattedIcEvT_Es_0= and
others, for =char= and =wchar_t=). The failure shows in the
prelinker's recompile only because that is where =test_formatted= is
instantiated.

A defect of the front end in strict mode, in every dialect: a string
literal of an inline function or a template instance is defined once
for each C-style cast of it to =const signed char*= or
=const unsigned char*= passed as a function argument, and every
definition has the same name. The =SCH= and =UCH= macros of the test
make such casts. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/243.

Fix: EDG's. The test needs no change.

Check: =27.ostream= builds under =eccp -A= with a front end that fixes
it.

** WAITING glibc's =signbit= assumes a GNU built-in in C++

glibc's =<math.h>= gives a C++ compiler that is neither GCC 6+ nor
Clang 3.3+ ~#define signbit(x) __builtin_signbitl (x)~. A compiler
that does not emulate GCC has no such built-in: under =eccp= with the
C99 names requested, =src/fpclass.h= fails. A regression in glibc
2.27, from the fix for its bug 22296. Reported as
https://sourceware.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=34717.

Without the C99 names, as in C++03 with no feature macro, glibc
defines no =fpclassify= and =src/fpclass.h= takes its stub branch:
=__rw_signbit=, =__rw_isnan= and =__rw_isinf= return false,
=__rw_isfinite= true. How the library classifies floating values
under a strict compiler is undecided; =signbit= is C99 and not in
C++03 either.

Fix: glibc's, for the header. The library side goes with the decision
on the C99 functions.

Check: =src/fpclass.h= compiles under =eccp -A= with the C99 names
requested and a glibc that fixes it.

* Documentation

Documents that describe the library as it is, for a reader, not for
the record. They go under =doc/notes= with the =ref-= prefix. Each
entry names the document, what it must answer and where the material
is. They are prose: no check beyond a reading. Roughly in order of
payback.

** TODO README section 5 and the configuration

The original README says to set =CPPOPTS= and =CXXOPTS= on the make
command line to change compiler options. The configuration does not
see them, by design. =config.h= is a function of the compiler, its
version and the build type; what the configuration must see goes in
the =CONFIG= file. A dialect option given only on the command line
configures one language and builds the library in another.

Fix: prose, no patch. The per-platform manual step the README
describes is a defect to move away from. =README.md= says so; the
original README stays as it is.

Check: none; this is prose.

** TODO A tour of the locale subsystem

